  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Consider the points $A(1,3)$, $B(3,9)$ and let $P(x,y)$ be a point.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find an expression for the gradients of $PA$ and $PB$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$m_{PA}=\\dfrac{y-3}{x-1}$, $m_{PB}=\\dfrac{y-9}{x-3}$, where the respective denominators are non-zero.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}m_{PA}&=\\frac{y-3}{x-1}\\\\m_{PB}&=\\frac{y-9}{x-3}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe respective gradients are undefined when $x=1$ or $x=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $\\angle APB=90^\\circ$. Show that $x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30=0$.",
          "worked": "For non-vertical $PA$ and $PB$, perpendicular gradients have product $-1$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}m_{PA}\\times m_{PB}&=-1\\\\\\frac{y-3}{x-1}\\times\\frac{y-9}{x-3}&=-1\\\\\\frac{(y-3)(y-9)}{(x-1)(x-3)}&=-1\\\\(y-3)(y-9)&=-(x-1)(x-3)\\\\y^2-12y+27&=-x^2+4x-3\\\\x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf one segment is vertical, the other is horizontal: $P=(1,9)$ or $P=(3,3)$. Both points also satisfy the final equation.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "By completing the square, show that this is the equation of a circle centred at $(2,6)$ with radius $\\sqrt{10}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2=10$; centre $(2,6)$, radius $\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}x^2-4x+y^2-12y+30&=0\\\\(x^2-4x+4)-4+(y^2-12y+36)-36+30&=0\\\\(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2-10&=0\\\\(x-2)^2+(y-6)^2&=10\\end{aligned}$$\n\nComparing with $(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2=r^2$, the centre is $(2,6)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{10}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p19-q6-d-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How is the point ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " related to the circle? A sketch using DESMOS may help.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p19-q6-d-link",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "https://www.desmos.com/geometry/opfcwyddok",
                  "marks": [],
                  "colour": "#268cff"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P$ lies on the circle with diameter $AB$, excluding $A$ and $B$.",
          "worked": "When $\\angle APB=90^\\circ$, $P$ lies on the circle centred at the midpoint of $AB$, with radius $\\sqrt{10}$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Midpoint of }AB&=\\left(\\frac{1+3}{2},\\frac{3+9}{2}\\right)=(2,6)\\\\\\frac{AB}{2}&=\\frac{\\sqrt{(3-1)^2+(9-3)^2}}{2}=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus $AB$ is a diameter. This is the circle theorem that the angle at the circumference in a semicircle is $90^\\circ$. The endpoints $A$ and $B$ are excluded because $\\angle APB$ would be undefined there.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      }
    ]
  },
